
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
-          inflateTT-MP - Yet Another RFC-1951 Decompressor           -
-                 Written by Ville Helin in 2007                      -
-----------------------------------------------------------------------


1. DESCRIPTION

inflateTT-MP is the portable DEFc decompressor. Literal, length and
distance symbols follow RFC 1951. The container is the DEFc layout
documented in the repository README, not a zlib or raw deflate stream.

The motivation behind writing this piece of code was to get a simple and
efficient data compression utility that didn't depend on any libraries,
like the pretty big and messy zlib, to my homebrew projects.
deflateTT is the compression tool that outputs files that can be
decompressed using inflateTT-MP.

Compile inflate.c into the project and call

  int inflate(const unsigned char *data, int dataSize,
              unsigned char *output, int outputCapacity,
              int *outputSize, struct InflateContext *context);

outputCapacity must hold the uncompressed size from the DEFc header.
Allocate InflateContext on the heap or in static storage; the node
pool makes it too large for a small stack. Return values are the
INFLATE_* codes in inflate.h.

Nintendo DS VRAM wants 16-bit stores. Compile inflate_ds.c instead of
inflate.c and call

  int inflate(const unsigned char *data, int dataSize,
              volatile unsigned short *output, int outputBytes);

output must be 2-byte aligned. outputBytes is the capacity in bytes.
This entry keeps one static context, so it is not re-entrant. Do not
link it with inflate.o. A caller that passes its own context uses
inflate_vram16 from inflate.c instead.


2. CONTACT

